# Joint after-tax accounts

Married plans can mark an after-tax account (`investment`, `annuity`, `cd`,
`savings`) as owned by both people. IRAs and Roths stay single-owner: they
cannot legally be joint, and `ownerId` drives RMDs and conversion eligibility.

## Model

`Account.joint` is optional. `ownerId` stays required as a fallback for
filing-status cleanup and deposit defaults. Joint is valid only when
`filingStatus === "mfj"` and the kind is after-tax (`canAccountBeJoint`).

The engine does not read `joint` or after-tax `ownerId`. Taxes, growth,
withdrawals, Medicare, and inheritance are unchanged.

## UI

On an MFJ after-tax card, Owner includes a **Both** option
(`JOINT_OWNER_VALUE`). Read view uses `accountOwnerLabel` ("Both" or the
person's name). Retirement and Roth cards stay person-only.

New deposits default `endYear` via `accountDepositEndYear`: the owner's
retirement year, or the later of the two when joint.

## Filing status and hydrate

`applyFilingStatus` to Single keeps joint after-tax accounts, clears `joint`,
and reassigns `ownerId` if it pointed at the removed spouse. Non-joint accounts
owned by the removed person are still dropped.

`migrateHousehold` / `healAccountJoint` strip `joint` from single-filer plans
and from non-after-tax accounts (including deleted accounts). Existing plans
without the flag are unchanged.

Revision history folds `joint` into Owner so a change reads as "Alex" → "Both",
not a separate Joint flag.
